<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Trinh lords</h1><p class="st-meta">1545 CE – 1787 CE · capital Thang Long</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The hereditary military rulers of northern Vietnam who held real power behind the Le emperors for over two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trinh Kiem</a>, son-in-law of the Le loyalist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim">Nguyen Kim</a>, took command of the Le restoration forces after Nguyen Kim's death in 1545 and became the real power behind the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-tung">Trinh Tung</a> recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thang Long</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mac-dynasty">Mac dynasty</a> in 1592.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Trinh lords governed Dang Ngoai, the northern realm Europeans called <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-protectorate-of-tonkin">Tonkin</a>, from a palace beside the Le emperor's, controlling the army, taxes and appointments while leaving ritual to the emperor. They fought seven campaigns against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> between 1627 and 1672 without success, then focused on administration, examinations and trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Peasant revolts weakened the north in the 18th century. In 1786 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tay Son</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quang-trung">Nguyen Hue</a> marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thang Long</a> and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-khai">Trinh Khai</a>, and the last lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-bong">Trinh Bong</a>, fled in 1787. Their realm passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tay-son-dynasty">Tay Son dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/trinh-lords.jpg" alt="Trinh lords" width="320" height="533"><figcaption>Image: Own work by uploader · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vietnam1650.GIF" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1545 CE – 1787 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thang Long</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Trinh Kiem, Trinh Tung, Trinh Sam</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Confucianism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dang Ngoai, Đàng Ngoài, Tonkin, Chúa Trịnh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1666&amp;focus=trinh-lords" aria-label="Trinh lords on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7610 1566 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Trinh lords lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7930 1822L7930 1822M7930 1822L7843 1596M7930 1822L7992 1921"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7930 1822h0M7843 1596h0M7992 1921h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7930 1822h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7930 1822h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1666&amp;focus=trinh-lords">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

